Z||000021||WKDFIRO||12-07-2000||05:30 PM||||Realisticly, you can put any engine you want as long as the physical dimensions of the engine and its components can be made to fit. The question is how bad do you want it to work? Do you want it to work bad enough go through the wiring nightmare of putting a Ford power plant and have gauges that work? Do you want to find out the amperage differences of a Toyota sensor and a GM tachometer? Sure, you can live without AC and without Cruise Control but do you really want to discover how Nissan speed sensors are different from the speedometer in your Fiero? How difficult can the temp gauge be?Wiring was never my thing so it just gives me the willies thinking about it. <P>But there those (God bless 'em!) That HAVE to be different.<P>It all comes down to how badly you want it in there!||||reg||10
Z||000022||rockcrawl||12-07-2000||06:13 PM|||| <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/~grannys/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.tripod.com/~grannys/index.html</A> <P>Here's a great site for engine swap info. I couldn't find anything on a Ford powered Fiero though! I know I've seen an SHO v-6 in a Fiero before, I believe there was a picture posted here on the forum. How 'bout a ZF transaxle (Pantera) with a 351 in a Fiero? I've seen pictures of that as well.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
